For autonomous driving applications, we adopt three directly applicable baselines: zero, uniform noise, and Gaussian noise. We conduct comparative experiments using these baselines, with uniform noise bounds and Gaussian parameters (mean and variance) derived from the dataset’s feature distribution. A critical requirement for baselines is their ability to represent feature absence in decision-making. For autonomous driving models, any baseline that induces a measurable decision shift satisfies this criterion. Building on this principle, we design an additional baseline using only the Lscore term from Eq. (10) for ablation studies. Our ablation study evaluates four baseline variants for the attribution path starting point: 1) Zero, 2) Uniform, 3) Gaussian, and 4) Lscore. As shown in Table I, all baselines yield suboptimal attribution results. Notably, the zero baseline outperforms other alternatives, suggesting that simpler baselines may better align with the feature absence criterion than introducing noise or Lscore baselines.
